    1: #!/usr/bin/env python3
    2: """DETECTOR for sh4gdromexit, carrying the round's witness measurement.
    3: 
    4: *** THE ARM HAS FIVE exit(1) SITES, NOT THREE, AND AN EARLIER DRAFT OF THIS
    5: DOCSTRING HID TWO BEHIND ELLIPSES. ***  :308 width, :315 read-with-no-data,
    6: :334 "Read too much from GDROM_DATA", :340 "Too much GDROM_DATA?", :352
    7: write-when-not-waiting.  A pass-1 seat caught it, and the count matters: the
    8: de-escalation clause enumerates EVERY predicate that used to gate death, so a
    9: false count makes that enumeration incomplete by construction.
   10: 
   11: THIS ROUND TAKES FOUR OF THE FIVE.  An earlier draft of this docstring said
   12: three and FILED :334, on the reading that witnessing it needed a new probe
   13: shape and a new oracle.  That was OVERTURNED BY MEASUREMENT: the existing
   14: session/run helpers plus a three-instruction loop under `step N` reach it --
   15: same rung, same oracle -- so it joined, and the `overread` rows below drive it.
   16: :315 and :334 are one predicate family ("read when there is nothing to give"),
   17: and fixing one while the other stayed fatal would leave a device where a
   18: declined read is safe in one state and lethal in another.
   19: 
   20: :340 is assessed UNREACHABLE and left alone: COND=0xa0 is the only setter of
   21: busy|0x08 and it zeroes cmd_count, GDROM_BUSY writes only clear, and cmd_count
   22: steps by 2 from 0 with ==12 clearing busy -- so >=12 with busy set has no path.
   23: Three constructed paths were measured against it and all reached :352.
   24: 
   25: *** THE SITE LINE NUMBERS ABOVE ARE PRE-FIX (at HEAD e4390ea). *** The fix adds
   26: lines above them, so they do not resolve in the post-fix tree -- the same trap
   27: this project hit twice in #459, recorded here rather than silently rotting.
   28: 
   29: THE FOUR THIS ROUND TAKES are separate `exit(1)` sites in ONE register's arm --
   30: GDROM_DATA, physical 0x005f7080 on the Dreamcast -- each reached by a SINGLE
   31: ordinary guest instruction on a freshly constructed machine.
   32: 
   33:   1. WIDTH.  `if (len != sizeof(uint16_t)) { fatal("Non-16bit GDROM data
   34:      access? TODO"); exit(1); }` -- so a byte or longword access, READ OR
   35:      WRITE, ends the host process.  No device state is required at all.
   36:   2. READ WITH NO DATA.  `if (!(d->cond & COND_DATA_AVAIL))` -> exit(1).
   37:      `cond` is zero on a fresh device, so the FIRST 16-bit read kills.
   38:   3. WRITE WHEN NOT EXPECTING DATA.  `if (d->busy & 0x08)` else -> exit(1).
   39:      `busy` is zero on a fresh device, so the FIRST 16-bit write kills.
   40: 
   41: So every access width and both directions are fatal on a fresh GDROM: there is
   42: no legal first touch of this register.
   43: 
   44: *** WHAT THAT DOES **NOT** MEAN, because an earlier draft overclaimed it. ***
   45: It does NOT mean a real driver cannot avoid this.  Drivers probe STAT, BUSY and
   46: COND -- not the data FIFO -- and the one driver this model was written against
   47: (NetBSD's dreamcast gdrom.c, cited by the device file itself) writes COND=0xa0
   48: before ever touching DATA, so ITS first touch is legal by construction.  The
   49: true statement is narrower and still severe: no device state makes a first
   50: touch of DATA legal, and any access outside the exact NetBSD-shaped sequence
   51: ends the host process.
   52: 
   53: *** DREAMCAST NEEDS -x OR IT NEVER REACHES THE DEBUGGER PROMPT. ***  It brings
   54: up TWO console inputs, "SH4 SCIF" and "maple", and console.c:994-1007 makes
   55: that fatal unless allow_slaves is set.  This cost #459 a false positive: the
   56: session died for a CONFIGURATION reason and printed the same "died" the defect
   57: prints.  -x only sets the flag (console.c:985 returns immediately) and spawns
   58: no xterm, because a cold-debugger probe never drives a slave console.
   59: 
   60: Rung 3 by the witness ladder: a real guest instruction through real address
   61: decode, on an UNMODIFIED in-tree machine description.  Nothing is added to
   62: reach the site.
   63: 
   64: CONTROLS RUN IN THEIR OWN SESSIONS AND NEVER TOUCH GDROM_DATA, so a red
   65: trigger row cannot be confused with a broken session.
   66: """
   67: import os
   68: import pty
   69: import re
   70: import select
   71: import sys
